Seborrheic dermatitis (adult)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Greasy yellow scales, Erythematous plaques, Seborrheic distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-036
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe facial and scalp scaling, resulting in intense pruritus, painful eyelid crusting, and extreme psychosocial distress preventing the patient from attending work.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an acute, explosive flare of intensely pruritic, weeping, and crusting facial lesions involving the eyelids (blepharitis) and causing severe psychosocial withdrawal. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out acute periocular cellulitis or severe eczema herpeticum, and to initiate immediate potent topical anti-inflammatory therapy to manage the severe ocular discomfort and psychosocial crisis before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face and scalp are completely covered in these gross, greasy yellow scabs. My eyelids are stuck together, and it burns so badly I can't even go outside."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of mild dandruff. Following a recent period of extreme psychological stress and the onset of dry winter weather, they experienced a sudden, severe exacerbation over the past 72 hours. The eruption consists of poorly marginated erythematous plaques covered heavily with thick, greasy yellow scales. The distribution is strongly localized to areas of high sebaceous gland density, specifically the scalp, nasolabial folds, eyebrows, and central chest. The patient is experiencing severe pruritus. Notably, they report acute blepharitis presenting as painful crusting along the eyelid margins, causing the eyes to stick shut upon waking.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently housebound due to severe psychosocial distress and ocular discomfort.
Social/Safety Concerns: High psychosocial distress; potential for ocular surface damage due to severe blepharitis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/84 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, constantly rubbing the face and eyes, severe visible scaling on dark clothing.
Physical Exam: The scalp demonstrates diffuse, thick, adherent yellow scaling with underlying erythema. The bilateral nasolabial folds, glabella, and medial eyebrows exhibit confluent, poorly marginated erythematous plaques covered in distinct greasy yellow scales. Examination of the eyes reveals severe bilateral anterior blepharitis with thick, greasy crusts adhering to the base of the eyelashes. The conjunctivae are mildly injected, but extraocular movements are intact and visual acuity is grossly normal. Similar erythematous, scaly plaques are present over the mid-sternum. There are no grouped clear vesicles, punched-out erosions, or spreading, hot, indurated cellulitic changes.
Environmental Observations: Clean home.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Seborrheic Dermatitis with acute anterior blepharitis - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Psoriasis (Facial/Scalp) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of greasy, yellow scale rather than thick, dry, silvery-white scale, and the classic involvement of the nasolabial folds and eyelids which is highly characteristic of seborrheic dermatitis.

Eczema Herpeticum - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of monomorphic, punched-out erosions, intact vesicles, and lack of systemic toxicity or high fever.

Rosacea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of significant scaling and the specific involvement of the nasolabial folds (rosacea typically spares the nasolabial folds and lacks greasy scale).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to psychosocial impact)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe flare of seborrheic dermatitis, heavily involving the periocular region. Triage accurately flagged the eyelid involvement and severe facial burning as requiring urgent assessment to rule out an acute infectious process threatening the eye (like periorbital cellulitis or herpetic keratitis). Immediate out-of-hours clinical evaluation confirmed the inflammatory, yeast-driven nature of the disease, ensuring the eyes were safe. By prescribing targeted, low-potency topical steroids for the face and specific eyelid hygiene measures, the intervention rapidly addressed the severe discomfort and provided the immediate clinical reassurance necessary to resolve the patient's acute psychosocial crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ketoconazole 2% shampoo to be used on the scalp and face (lathered and left for 5 minutes before rinsing) every 2-3 days. Prescribed Hydrocortisone 1% cream to be applied sparingly to the facial plaques BD for 5 days. Instructed on strict eyelid hygiene: using diluted baby shampoo on a cotton bud to mechanically gently debride the eyelash crusts BD.
Positive Behavioral Change: By using the gentle baby shampoo to carefully clean the crusts off your eyelashes every morning and night, you will physically remove the yeast and oil buildup, immediately relieving the painful burning and stopping your eyes from sticking together. By strictly avoiding picking or scratching the greasy scales on your face, you will allow the mild steroid cream to rapidly reduce the redness without introducing a secondary bacterial infection into the raw skin.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to assess the resolution of the blepharitis and transition to long-term maintenance therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If your eyes become severely red, if you experience sharp pain when looking at light, or if your vision becomes blurred, you must go to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Seborrheic dermatitis is a chronic, relapsing condition driven by a normal skin yeast; it cannot be permanently cured, but it can be controlled.
Clinical Course: The extreme itch and facial redness should significantly improve within 3-5 days of using the medicated shampoo and steroid cream.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your eyes closely. Any changes in your actual vision or severe pain inside the eye requires immediate emergency review.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A normal yeast that lives on your skin has massively overgrown due to stress and winter weather, triggering a severe allergic-type reaction in your oil glands. The shampoo kills the yeast, and the mild steroid cream acts like a fire extinguisher to put out the intense redness and itch.
Actionable Steps: Only use the steroid cream for a maximum of 5 to 7 days on the face to prevent skin thinning. Continue using the medicated shampoo long-term to prevent relapses.

